powerpagecomponent.content:
{
  "source": "{% extends 'OT Layout' %}
{% block content %}
{% comment %}
  Review detail: renders the questionnaire version issued to this review
  (PP-07, PP-08, PP-09).

  The chain is Checklist Version -> Section -> Question -> Question Version.
  Questions are read from the checklist version stamped on the review instance,
  and of each question's versions only the one in force on the review's
  reference day renders: the submission day once submitted, otherwise today
  (AD-015, BR-013, FR-030, FR-031). A retired version keeps its effective-to
  date and stays Active, so without that filter every version of a question
  rendered at once - which is how a submitted Tax review came to show Tax check
  reason three times and hold an answer on each. The server applies the same
  rule (ResponseRules.IsVersionEffective) to the submit gate and the outcome.

  Answerable by the assigned checker (AD-053). Both writes on this page go through an
  allowlisted trigger column and a synchronous plug-in rather than the Web API's own
  create/update/action verbs, for two different reasons. Answering PATCHes a JSON payload
  onto al_reviewinstance.al_answerrequest, because Power Pages refuses the al_response
  create's @odata.bind association - and a fail reason's $ref associate - with error
  90040106 regardless of table permissions; AnswerRequestPlugin parses the payload and
  AnswerWriter does the al_response create/update instead. Submitting PATCHes
  al_reviewinstance.al_submitrequested, because the Portals Web API cannot invoke the
  al_SubmitReview Custom API at all; SubmitRequestPlugin does that work instead of a Power
  Automate flow. Every rule that matters - the submission lock, the AD-023 column match,
  the option subset and AD-020 section ownership - is enforced by ResponseGuardPlugin, so
  nothing here is a security boundary (NFR-SEC-01).
{% endcomment %}

{% assign review_id = request.params['id'] %}

{% comment %}
  A claim lands here by case rather than by review id: the queue page cannot read the new
  instance's id over the Web API (the al_reviewinstance allowlist carries only the two
  trigger columns, and widening it would hand the reviewer the status column), but Liquid
  can, with no allowlist in the way. The open instance of that discipline assigned to the
  signed-in user is the one just created; if none is found the page reads as "no review
  selected", which is what a stale link should say.
{% endcomment %}
{% if review_id == null or review_id == empty %}
  {% assign case_param = request.params['case'] %}
  {% assign type_param = request.params['type'] %}
  {% if user and case_param and case_param != empty %}
    {% fetchxml by_case %}
    <fetch top="1">
      <entity name="al_reviewinstance">
        <attribute name="al_reviewinstanceid" />
        <filter type="and">
          <condition attribute="al_outcomecaseid" operator="eq" value="{{ case_param | xml_escape }}" />
          <condition attribute="al_assignedcontactid" operator="eq" value="{{ user.id }}" />
          <condition attribute="al_submittedon" operator="null" />
          {% if type_param and type_param != empty %}
          <condition attribute="al_reviewtype" operator="eq" value="{{ type_param | xml_escape }}" />
          {% endif %}
        </filter>
        <order attribute="createdon" descending="true" />
      </entity>
    </fetch>
    {% endfetchxml %}
    {% assign by_case_row = by_case.results.entities[0] %}
    {% if by_case_row %}
      {% assign review_id = by_case_row.al_reviewinstanceid %}
    {% endif %}
  {% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if review_id == null or review_id == empty %}
  {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'No review selected' message: 'Open a review from a worklist to see its questionnaire.' %}
{% else %}

  {% fetchxml review %}
  <fetch top="1">
    <entity name="al_reviewinstance">
      <attribute name="al_reviewinstanceid" />
      <attribute name="al_name" />
      <attribute name="al_reviewtype" />
      <attribute name="al_reviewstatus" />
      <attribute name="al_sequence" />
      <attribute name="al_startedon" />
      <attribute name="al_submittedon" />
      <attribute name="al_checklistversionid" />
      <attribute name="al_assignedcontactid" />
      <filter type="and">
        <condition attribute="al_reviewinstanceid" operator="eq" value="{{ review_id | xml_escape }}" />
      </filter>
      <link-entity name="al_outcomecase" from="al_outcomecaseid" to="al_outcomecaseid" link-type="outer" alias="case">
        <attribute name="al_outcomecaseid" />
        <attribute name="al_casereference" />
        <attribute name="al_clientname" />
        <attribute name="al_advisername" />
        <attribute name="al_adviserstatus" />
        <attribute name="al_advisercode" />
        <attribute name="al_paraplanner" />
        <attribute name="al_paraplannercode" />
        <attribute name="al_products" />
        <attribute name="al_casetype" />
        <attribute name="al_advicedate" />
        <attribute name="al_productsolutiontype" />
        <attribute name="al_samplesource" />
        <attribute name="al_checkername" />
        <attribute name="al_checkdate" />
        <attribute name="al_preorpostcheck" />
        <attribute name="al_vulnerableclient" />
        <attribute name="al_taxcheckrequired" />
        <attribute name="al_taxteamdisposition" />
      </link-entity>
    </entity>
  </fetch>
  {% endfetchxml %}

  {% assign rv = review.results.entities[0] %}

  {% if rv == null %}
    {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'Review not available' message: 'This review does not exist, or you do not have permission to view it.' %}
  {% else %}

    {% assign cv_id = rv.al_checklistversionid.id %}

    {% comment %}
      AD-020 section ownership, keyed by the review's discipline. Filtering in the query
      rather than in the loop is what makes the Tax/AQS split structural: a Tax checker's
      page never receives an AQS section's questions at all, which is what PP-08's
      "no cross-discipline overwrite" needs. The same rule is enforced again server-side
      by ResponseGuardPlugin, because a filter in a page is not a security boundary
      (NFR-SEC-01).
    {% endcomment %}
    {% if rv.al_reviewtype.value == 120910200 %}
      {% assign owner_role = 120910100 %}
    {% else %}
      {% assign owner_role = 120910101 %}
    {% endif %}

    {% comment %}
      A submitted review is read-only (PP-11). The questionnaire renders for a submitted
      review too, so every control is disabled and the autosave script is not emitted at
      all. ResponseGuardPlugin refuses the write regardless; disabling the control is what
      stops a checker typing into a field that can never save.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% if rv.al_submittedon %}
      {% assign locked = true %}
    {% else %}
      {% assign locked = false %}
    {% endif %}

    {% comment %}
      AD-056: a reviewer reads every review but changes only their own. Because reading
      another reviewer's review is now intended, the page must say so and disable its
      controls - otherwise a checker types into fields that the table permissions will
      refuse on save. The permission chain is the actual boundary; this is what stops the
      user discovering it the hard way.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% if rv.al_assignedcontactid.id and rv.al_assignedcontactid.id == user.id %}
      {% assign mine = true %}
    {% else %}
      {% assign mine = false %}
    {% endif %}

    {% if locked or mine == false %}
      {% assign editable = false %}
    {% else %}
      {% assign editable = true %}
    {% endif %}

    <section class="ot-card" aria-labelledby="ot-review-summary">
      <h2 class="ot-card__title" id="ot-review-summary">
        {{ rv.al_reviewtype.label | escape }} review
        {% include 'OT Status Badge' status: rv.al_reviewstatus.label %}
      </h2>
      <dl class="ot-dl">
        <div>
          <dt>Case</dt>
          <dd>
            {% assign cid = rv['case.al_outcomecaseid'] %}
            {% if cid %}
              <a href="/case-details?id={{ cid }}">{{ rv['case.al_casereference'] | default: 'Untitled case' | escape }}</a>
            {% else %}
              {{ rv['case.al_casereference'] | default: '—' | escape }}
            {% endif %}
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div><dt>Client</dt><dd>{{ rv['case.al_clientname'] | default: '—' | escape }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Adviser</dt><dd>{{ rv['case.al_advisername'] | default: '—' | escape }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Checklist version</dt><dd>{{ rv.al_checklistversionid.name | default: '—' | escape }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Sequence</dt><dd>{{ rv.al_sequence | default: '—' }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Started</dt><dd>{{ rv.al_startedon | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' | default: '—' }}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Submitted</dt><dd>{{ rv.al_submittedon | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' | default: '—' }}</dd></div>
      </dl>

      {% comment %}
        Keeping a copy of the checklist. The browser's print dialogue is the export - every
        platform's offers "Save as PDF" - and the print rules in outcome-testing.css strip
        the chrome so what lands in the file is this page's questionnaire and answers.

        No PDF library, for two reasons: Power Pages serves scripts only from an allowlisted
        set of CDNs, and a generated document would be a second description of this layout to
        keep in step with the one people read. The button itself is .ot-btn, which the print
        rules hide, so it never appears in the output.

        Offered on a submitted review only. What this exports is a record of the check, and
        before submission there is no check to record - the answers are still being edited,
        so a file saved from here would be a draft that reads exactly like a final one and
        carries no submission date to say otherwise. The button appears the moment the
        review is submitted and the page turns read-only.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% if rv.al_submittedon %}
        <p class="ot-print-action">
          <button type="button" class="ot-btn" data-ot-print>Save as PDF</button>
        </p>

        <script>
          (function () {
            'use strict';
            var button = document.querySelector('[data-ot-print]');
            if (!button) { return; }
            button.addEventListener('click', function () { window.print(); });
          })();
        </script>
      {% endif %}
    </section>

    {% if rv.al_submittedon %}
      <div class="ot-notice" role="status">
        <strong>Submitted {{ rv.al_submittedon | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' }}.</strong>
        This review is locked. Reopening or regrading is a T&amp;C Manager action and
        requires a recorded reason (AD-031).
      </div>
    {% elsif mine == false %}
      {% comment %}
        Reading another reviewer's work is intended under AD-056; changing it is not.
        Saying so plainly is better than presenting controls that fail on save.
      {% endcomment %}
      <div class="ot-notice" role="status">
        <strong>This review is assigned to someone else.</strong>
        You can read it, but only the assigned reviewer can record or change answers on it.
      </div>
    {% endif %}

    {% comment %}
      The document's header block (AD-098): the case fields in the order the Checker
      Checklist lays them out. Outcome Case columns captured at intake, not questions; read
      only here, as on the checklist itself. Choice columns come through the alias as
      option objects, so their label is read rather than their value.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% assign h_adviserstatus = rv['case.al_adviserstatus'] %}
    {% assign h_casetype = rv['case.al_casetype'] %}
    {% assign h_producttype = rv['case.al_productsolutiontype'] %}
    {% assign h_samplesource = rv['case.al_samplesource'] %}
    {% assign h_prepost = rv['case.al_preorpostcheck'] %}
    {% assign h_vulnerable = rv['case.al_vulnerableclient'] %}
    {% assign h_taxrequired = rv['case.al_taxcheckrequired'] %}
    {% assign h_taxdisposition = rv['case.al_taxteamdisposition'] %}
    {% comment %}
      The case header, held rather than emitted: on the document it is the first block of the
      form, so it is written inside the checklist wrapper below rather than as a card of its
      own above it. Outcome Case columns captured at intake, read only here as on the
      checklist itself. Choice columns come through the alias as option objects, so their
      label is read rather than their value.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% capture case_header %}
      <table class="meta">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="lbl">Adviser name</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_advisername'] | escape }}</td><td class="lbl">Adviser status</td><td class="val">{{ h_adviserstatus.label | escape }}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="lbl">Adviser code</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_advisercode'] | escape }}</td><td class="lbl">Paraplanner</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_paraplanner'] | escape }}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="lbl">Paraplanner code</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_paraplannercode'] | escape }}</td><td class="lbl">Product(s)</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_products'] | escape }}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="lbl">Case type</td><td class="val">{{ h_casetype.label | escape }}</td><td class="lbl">Advice date</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_advicedate'] | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' }}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="lbl">Product / solution type</td><td class="val">{{ h_producttype.label | escape }}</td><td class="lbl">Sample source</td><td class="val">{{ h_samplesource.label | escape }}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="lbl">Checker name</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_checkername'] | escape }}</td><td class="lbl">Check date</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_checkdate'] | date: 'dd MMM yyyy' }}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="lbl">Client name / initials</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_clientname'] | escape }}</td><td class="lbl">IO reference</td><td class="val">{{ rv['case.al_casereference'] | escape }}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="lbl">Pre or post check</td><td class="val">{{ h_prepost.label | escape }}</td><td class="lbl">Vulnerable client?</td><td class="val">{{ h_vulnerable.label | escape }}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="lbl">Tax check required<br>(Tax team to complete)</td><td class="val">{{ h_taxrequired.label | escape }}</td><td class="lbl">For Tax team usage</td><td class="val">{{ h_taxdisposition.label | escape }}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    {% endcapture %}

    {% if cv_id == null %}
      {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'No checklist version issued' message: 'This review has no checklist version, so no questions can be rendered.' %}
    {% else %}

      {% comment %}
        The day this review's questions are read against. Effective-from and
        effective-to are date-only columns, and RetireAndSucceedQuestion stamps the old
        version's effective-to and the successor's effective-from with the same date, so
        "in force" is from <= day < to.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% if rv.al_submittedon %}
        {% assign as_of = rv.al_submittedon | date: 'yyyy-MM-dd' %}
      {% else %}
        {% assign as_of = now | date: 'yyyy-MM-dd' %}
      {% endif %}

      {% fetchxml questions %}
      <fetch>
        <entity name="al_questionversion">
          <attribute name="al_questionversionid" />
          <attribute name="al_questiontext" />
          <attribute name="al_responsetype" />
          <attribute name="al_ismandatory" />
          <attribute name="al_versionnumber" />
          <attribute name="al_displayorder" />
          <filter type="and">
            <filter type="or">
              <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="null" />
              <condition attribute="al_effectivefrom" operator="on-or-before" value="{{ as_of }}" />
            </filter>
            <filter type="or">
              <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="null" />
              <condition attribute="al_effectiveto" operator="gt" value="{{ as_of }}" />
            </filter>
          </filter>
          <link-entity name="al_question" from="al_questionid" to="al_questionid" alias="q">
            <attribute name="al_displayorder" />
            <attribute name="al_questioncode" />
            <link-entity name="al_section" from="al_sectionid" to="al_sectionid" alias="s">
              <attribute name="al_sectionid" />
              <attribute name="al_name" />
              <attribute name="al_displayorder" />
              <attribute name="al_helptext" />
              <attribute name="al_ownerrole" />
              <attribute name="al_sectioncode" />
              <filter type="and">
                <condition attribute="al_checklistversionid" operator="eq" value="{{ cv_id }}" />
                <condition attribute="al_ownerrole" operator="eq" value="{{ owner_role }}" />
              </filter>
            </link-entity>
          </link-entity>
          <order entityname="s" attribute="al_displayorder" />
          <order entityname="q" attribute="al_displayorder" />
        </entity>
      </fetch>
      {% endfetchxml %}

      {% comment %}
        The sections this team owns, in the document's order - fetched separately, and the
        thing the questionnaire below actually iterates.

        The questions fetch above asks for section-then-question order with two root-level
        order elements carrying an entityname. The SDK honours that; this page's Liquid
        fetch does not - it applied only the question order, so every section's question 1
        came back before any section's question 2 and the whole form arrived interleaved
        (project owner's Tax and AQS PDFs, 2026-09-09). The rows are grouped by section here
        instead of relying on that ordering: this fetch's order is a plain root-entity order
        on al_section, which is applied, and within one section the question order from the
        fetch above is applied too. So the sequence no longer depends on cross-entity
        ordering working. Leave the orders above in place - they cost nothing and they are
        what the SDK and any future engine will use.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% fetchxml sections %}
      <fetch>
        <entity name="al_section">
          <attribute name="al_sectionid" />
          <attribute name="al_sectioncode" />
          <attribute name="al_displayorder" />
          <filter type="and">
            <condition attribute="al_checklistversionid" operator="eq" value="{{ cv_id | xml_escape }}" />
            <condition attribute="al_ownerrole" operator="eq" value="{{ owner_role }}" />
          </filter>
          <order attribute="al_displayorder" />
        </entity>
      </fetch>
      {% endfetchxml %}

      {% fetchxml responses %}
      <fetch>
        <entity name="al_response">
          <attribute name="al_responseid" />
          <attribute name="al_questionversionid" />
          <attribute name="al_answertext" />
          <attribute name="al_answerchoice" />
          <attribute name="al_answerchoices" />
          <attribute name="al_answerdate" />
          <filter type="and">
            <condition attribute="al_reviewinstanceid" operator="eq" value="{{ review_id | xml_escape }}" />
          </filter>
        </entity>
      </fetch>
      {% endfetchxml %}

      {% comment %}
        Fail reasons (FR-013, AD-096). Reference data, fetched once, for the standalone
        File Quality fail points block rendered before the File Quality outcome section.

        Both teams get the whole list. The document draws one undivided twenty-row table, and
        the category is a grouping of the reasons rather than a split of who may pick them
        (project owner, 2026-09-09). This used to filter by category - Tax check to the Tax
        team, the other three to AQS - which was an inference from the prefixes, and it left a
        Tax reviewer who found a record-keeping failure with nowhere to record it.

        The two teams' ticks still cannot collide: each hangs off that team's own File quality
        outcome answer (Q-FQ-01 for AQS, Q-FQTAX-01 for Tax) through the response-keyed
        intersect, and ReconcileFailReasons only ever touches ids this page rendered.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% fetchxml failreasons %}
      <fetch>
        <entity name="al_failreason">
          <attribute name="al_failreasonid" />
          <attribute name="al_name" />
          <attribute name="al_category" />
          <attribute name="al_displayorder" />
          <order attribute="al_displayorder" />
        </entity>
      </fetch>
      {% endfetchxml %}

      {% fetchxml selectedreasons %}
      <fetch>
        <entity name="al_failreason">
          <attribute name="al_failreasonid" />
          <link-entity name="al_al_failreason_al_response" from="al_failreasonid" to="al_failreasonid" intersect="true">
            <link-entity name="al_response" from="al_responseid" to="al_responseid" alias="r">
              <attribute name="al_responseid" />
              <filter type="and">
                <condition attribute="al_reviewinstanceid" operator="eq" value="{{ review_id | xml_escape }}" />
              </filter>
            </link-entity>
          </link-entity>
        </entity>
      </fetch>
      {% endfetchxml %}

      {% comment %}
        Flattened to a delimited key list so the per-checkbox test is a string match
        rather than a nested loop over every pair for all 42 questions.
      {% endcomment %}
      {% capture selected_reason_pairs %}{% for s in selectedreasons.results.entities %}|{{ s['r.al_responseid'] }}~{{ s.al_failreasonid }}{% endfor %}|{% endcapture %}

      {% assign qrows = questions.results.entities %}
      {% assign arows = responses.results.entities %}

      {% if qrows.size == 0 %}
        {% include 'OT Empty State' title: 'No questions in this version' message: 'The checklist version issued to this review has no sections or questions.' %}
      {% else %}

        {% comment %}
          The questionnaire. Each question renders the control matching its response
          type and writes the AD-023 typed column. The wrapper carries the review id so
          the autosave script does not have to re-parse the query string.
        {% endcomment %}
        <div class="ot-checklist" data-ot-questionnaire data-review-id="{{ rv.al_reviewinstanceid }}">
        <div class="doc-footer">Outcome Testing Checker Checklist | V5 Draft</div>
        <h1>Outcome Testing - Checker Checklist</h1>
        {{ case_header }}
        {% comment %}
          The document's blocks (AD-098). The checklist model holds Section then Question
          with no grouping level, but the Checker Checklist lays its sections out under its
          own headings: E1 to E5 together under "Suitability core checks" as one table headed
          "Suitability test point", the Tax check as "File Quality - Tax check section", and
          so on. So each section is read into the document's block by its code, here and in
          the app (checklistForm.ts, formBlocks), and a card opens per block rather than per
          section. A section the document does not know keeps its own name.

          A grid block renders its questions as rows of one table with a radio per tick
          column, which is the document's layout. The row itself is the autosave root
          (data-ot-answer): the radios sit in the tick cells and the status line in the
          question cell, so bind() finds everything it needs inside the row exactly as it
          did inside the old div. A question whose response type is not the block's scale
          spans the tick columns with its usual control.
        {% endcomment %}
        {% assign current_blk = '' %}
        {% assign current_section = '' %}
        {% assign blk_layout = 'inline' %}
        {% assign prev_help = '' %}
        {% assign ncols = 2 %}
        {% comment %}
          The tick box the document draws on E2's outcome lens row, and on no other. It is a
          question of its own (Q-E2-LENS, display order last in its section), so it is picked
          up here as the loop passes it and held until the lens row is written - which happens
          once the section is finished, so the capture is always ready in time. Recognised by
          the -LENS code rather than by the section, matching checklistForm.ts isLensTick.
        {% endcomment %}
        {% assign lens_tick_id = '' %}
        {% assign lens_tick_cell = '' %}
        {% assign lens_tick_span = 0 %}
        {% comment %}
          Sections outer, that section's questions inner. The rows are matched on the section
          CODE rather than the section id, because the code is a plain string on both sides
          while an aliased guid's type through Liquid is not something to bet the whole form
          on - and the code is already what every block decision below is made from.

          first_row replaces forloop.first: the loop that opens and closes the block tables is
          no longer the outer one, so "have we opened a table yet" has to be state rather than
          a loop position. The last block is closed after both loops for the same reason.
        {% endcomment %}
        {% assign first_row = true %}
        {% for sec in sections.results.entities %}
        {% for qv in qrows %}
          {% if qv['s.al_sectioncode'] == sec.al_sectioncode %}
          {% assign sid = qv['s.al_sectionid'] %}
          {% assign scode = qv['s.al_sectioncode'] %}
          {% assign sname = qv['s.al_name'] %}
          {% assign shelp = qv['s.al_helptext'] %}

          {% comment %}
            The block this section belongs to. Every section carries a code, so the fallback
            is only there for one seeded without.

            This was `scode | default: sid`, and it is an explicit if now only because the
            variable it assigned used to be called `block` - a name this engine will not let
            you assign, the page body being inside a block tag - so it never varied, the
            boundary below fired once for the whole form, and the fail points block, which
            hangs off `blk == 'S-FQ'`, never rendered at all. The rename to blk is the fix.
            Nothing here proves the `default` filter was at fault: the summary card above uses
            it half a dozen times and renders correctly. It is written out longhand because an
            if cannot be misread, not because the filter was found guilty of anything.
          {% endcomment %}
          {% if scode != blank %}
            {% assign blk = scode %}
          {% else %}
            {% assign blk = sid %}
          {% endif %}
          {% assign blk_title = sname %}
          {% assign blk_intro = shelp %}
          {% assign layout = 'inline' %}
          {% assign grid_rt = 0 %}
          {% assign grid_col = 'Check' %}
          {% assign subsections = false %}
          {% comment %}
            Written as if / elsif on one value at a time, deliberately.

            This was a case block on scode with two multi-value whens - S-FQOUT or S-FQTAX,
            and S-E1 through S-E5. Multi-value "when" is an extension, not core Liquid, and
            when it does not match there is no error: the case simply falls through, every E
            section keeps its own name and inline layout, and the document's "Suitability
            core checks" / "Suitability test point" table never appears at all. That is
            precisely the symptom the project owner reported twice (AD-098, and again
            2026-09-09), so the construct is gone rather than trusted. One test per code.

            NOTE FOR ANY COMMENT IN THIS FILE: never write Liquid tag delimiters inside one.
            This engine parses the contents of a comment block, so a tag named in passing is
            opened for real - and an unclosed one turns the endcomment into "Unknown tag
            'endcomment'" and takes the whole page down. Name tags in prose, without braces.
          {% endcomment %}
          {% if scode == 'S-TAX' %}
            {% assign blk_title = 'File Quality - Tax check section' %}
          {% elsif scode == 'S-AMLCRA' %}
            {% assign blk_title = 'File Quality - AML and CRA checking points' %}
            {% assign layout = 'grid' %}{% assign grid_rt = 120910008 %}
          {% elsif scode == 'S-FQOUT' %}
            {% assign blk = 'S-FQ' %}
            {% assign blk_title = 'File Quality Outcome' %}
          {% elsif scode == 'S-FQTAX' %}
            {% assign blk = 'S-FQ' %}
            {% assign blk_title = 'File Quality Outcome' %}
          {% elsif scode == 'S-E1' or scode == 'S-E2' or scode == 'S-E3' or scode == 'S-E4' or scode == 'S-E5' %}
            {% assign blk = 'S-E' %}
            {% assign blk_title = 'Suitability core checks' %}
            {% assign blk_intro = 'Suitability core checks are shown in a consistent Pass/Fail format against each test point.' %}
            {% assign layout = 'grid' %}{% assign grid_rt = 120910006 %}{% assign grid_col = 'Suitability test point' %}
            {% assign subsections = true %}
          {% elsif scode == 'S-CRP' %}
            {% assign blk_title = 'Centralised Retirement Proposition' %}
            {% assign blk_intro = 'Complete this section where retirement income planning or decumulation advice is in scope.' %}
            {% assign layout = 'grid' %}{% assign grid_rt = 120910006 %}{% assign grid_col = 'Centralised Retirement Proposition test point' %}
          {% elsif scode == 'S-CD' %}
            {% assign blk_title = 'Consumer Duty overlay' %}
            {% assign layout = 'grid' %}{% assign grid_rt = 120910009 %}{% assign grid_col = 'Outcome' %}
          {% elsif scode == 'S-GRADE' %}
            {% assign blk_title = 'Checker judgement and grading' %}
          {% endif %}

          {% comment %}
            The tick columns for whichever scale this block is headed by. A `case` on an
            integer is the same gamble as the one above, so this is an if chain too.
          {% endcomment %}
          {% if grid_rt == 120910006 %}
            {% assign gvalues = '120910300,120910301,120910302' | split: ',' %}
            {% assign glabels = 'Pass,Fail,Insufficient evidence' | split: ',' %}
          {% elsif grid_rt == 120910008 %}
            {% assign gvalues = '120910305,120910306,120910307' | split: ',' %}
            {% assign glabels = 'Yes,No,N/A' | split: ',' %}
          {% elsif grid_rt == 120910009 %}
            {% assign gvalues = '120910305,120910306,120910302' | split: ',' %}
            {% assign glabels = 'Yes,No,Insufficient evidence' | split: ',' %}
          {% endif %}

          {% comment %}
            The document's blocks, drawn as the document draws them: an h2, the intro line
            where there is one, then one table. No cards, no badges, no scroll boxes - the
            page around this keeps the site's brand, the form itself is the V8 checklist.

            Three table shapes, matching the reference build:
              .grid  - a test-point table, 55% label plus one 15% tick column per scale value
              .meta  - a label / value table, 17% label plus the control across the rest
              .fails - the fail points table, 90% reason plus a 10% Tick column
          {% endcomment %}
          {% if blk != current_blk %}
            {% unless first_row %}
              {% if blk_subsections and prev_help != blank %}
                <tr class="lens"{% if lens_tick_id != '' %} data-ot-answer data-question-version="{{ lens_tick_id }}" data-response-type="120910007" data-column="choice"{% endif %}><td colspan="{{ ncols | minus: lens_tick_span }}"><em>Outcome lens:</em> {{ prev_help | escape }}</td>{{ lens_tick_cell }}</tr>{% assign lens_tick_id = '' %}{% assign lens_tick_cell = '' %}{% assign lens_tick_span = 0 %}
              {% endif %}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            {% endunless %}
            {% assign current_blk = blk %}
            {% assign current_section = '' %}
            {% assign blk_layout = layout %}
            {% assign blk_subsections = subsections %}
            {% assign blk_rt = grid_rt %}
            {% if layout == 'grid' %}
              {% assign ncols = gvalues.size | plus: 1 %}
            {% else %}
              {% assign ncols = 4 %}
            {% endif %}

            {% comment %}
              File Quality - Fail points (AD-096, superseding AD-054). On the document this
              is its own block, between the checking points and the File Quality outcome,
              and it is rendered as one here: the document's two-column table of every
              reason against a Tick box, the same table the app draws (checklistForm.ts,
              failPoints), not a picker under every non-pass answer. The ticks are recorded
              against the File quality outcome answer - the first question of the outcome
              block, which is what qv is at this boundary - because the intersect is
              response-keyed (AD-025) and that is the answer these reasons explain. The
              outcome question's autosave binds the list through data-ot-failpoints-for, so
              a tick saves like any other change to that answer.
            {% endcomment %}
            {% if blk == 'S-FQ' %}
              {% assign fq_existing = null %}
              {% for a in arows %}
                {% if a.al_questionversionid.id == qv.al_questionversionid %}{% assign fq_existing = a %}{% endif %}
              {% endfor %}
              <h2>File Quality &ndash; Fail points</h2>
              <table class="fails" data-ot-failpoints-for="{{ qv.al_questionversionid }}">
                <thead>
                  <tr>
                    <th class="label">File Quality fail reason</th>
                    <th class="tick">Tick</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {% for fr in failreasons.results.entities %}
                    <tr>
                      <td class="label"><label for="fr-{{ fr.al_failreasonid }}">{{ fr.al_name | escape }}</label></td>
                      <td class="tick">{% capture pairkey %}|{{ fq_existing.al_responseid }}~{{ fr.al_failreasonid }}{% endcapture %}<input type="checkbox" class="cc-box" id="fr-{{ fr.al_failreasonid }}" data-ot-reason value="{{ fr.al_failreasonid }}"{% unless editable %} disabled{% endunless %}{% if fq_existing.al_responseid and selected_reason_pairs contains pairkey %} checked{% endif %} /></td>
                    </tr>
                  {% endfor %}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            {% endif %}

            <h2>{{ blk_title | escape }}</h2>
            {% if blk_intro != blank %}
              <p class="intro">{{ blk_intro | escape }}</p>
            {% endif %}
            {% if layout == 'grid' %}
            <table class="grid">
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th class="label">{{ grid_col }}</th>
                  {% for l in glabels %}<th class="opt">{{ l }}</th>{% endfor %}
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
            {% else %}
            <table class="meta">
              <tbody>
            {% endif %}
          {% endif %}

          {% if sid != current_section %}
            {% if blk_subsections %}
              {% if current_section != '' and prev_help != blank %}
                <tr class="lens"{% if lens_tick_id != '' %} data-ot-answer data-question-version="{{ lens_tick_id }}" data-response-type="120910007" data-column="choice"{% endif %}><td colspan="{{ ncols | minus: lens_tick_span }}"><em>Outcome lens:</em> {{ prev_help | escape }}</td>{{ lens_tick_cell }}</tr>{% assign lens_tick_id = '' %}{% assign lens_tick_cell = '' %}{% assign lens_tick_span = 0 %}
              {% endif %}
              <tr class="section"><td colspan="{{ ncols }}">{{ scode | remove_first: 'S-' }}. {{ sname | escape }}</td></tr>
            {% endif %}
            {% assign current_section = sid %}
            {% assign prev_help = shelp %}
          {% endif %}

          {% assign qv_id = qv.al_questionversionid %}
          {% assign rt = qv.al_responsetype.value %}

          {% assign existing = null %}
          {% for a in arows %}
            {% if a.al_questionversionid.id == qv_id %}{% assign existing = a %}{% endif %}
          {% endfor %}

          {% if rt == 120910000 or rt == 120910001 %}{% assign column = 'text' %}
          {% elsif rt == 120910002 %}{% assign column = 'date' %}
          {% elsif rt == 120910004 %}{% assign column = 'choices' %}
          {% else %}{% assign column = 'choice' %}
          {% endif %}

          {% comment %}
            A ticked answer carries no free-text box. The Checker Checklist gives a tick
            column and nothing else against each test point, and the only free text the
            document asks for is its own questions - Case notes, Fail observation, Even
            Better If... - which render as multiline questions in their own right. The
            "Evidence or observation" box that used to sit under every non-pass answer was
            never on the form, so it is gone, and the reveal rule that opened it with it.

            The fail reasons used to sit here too, under every non-pass answer (AD-054).
            They are one standalone block now (AD-096).
          {% endcomment %}
          {% capture chosen_value %}{{ existing.al_answerchoice.value }}{% endcapture %}

          {% assign qcode = qv['q.al_questioncode'] %}
          {% if qcode contains '-LENS' %}
            {% comment %}
              One box, not a Yes / No pair, because that is what the document draws: ticked
              records Yes (120910305), untouched records nothing. The lens row itself becomes
              the autosave root, so bind() finds this input and saves it like any other answer
              - there is no status line on that row, and show() already tolerates its absence.
            {% endcomment %}
            {% assign lens_tick_id = qv_id %}
            {% assign lens_tick_span = 1 %}
            {% capture lens_tick_cell %}<td class="optcell"><input type="checkbox" class="cc-box" value="120910305" data-ot-input aria-label="{{ qv.al_questiontext | escape }}"{% unless editable %} disabled{% endunless %}{% if chosen_value == '120910305' %} checked{% endif %} /></td>{% endcapture %}

          {% elsif blk_layout == 'grid' and rt == blk_rt %}
            <tr data-ot-answer
                data-question-version="{{ qv_id }}"
                data-response-id="{{ existing.al_responseid }}"
                data-response-type="{{ rt }}"
                data-column="choice">
              <td class="label">{{ qv.al_questiontext | escape }}{% if qv.al_ismandatory %}<span class="ot-required">Required</span>{% endif %}<span class="ot-answer__status" data-ot-status aria-live="polite"></span></td>
              {% for v in gvalues %}
                <td class="optcell"><input type="radio" class="cc-box" name="q-{{ qv_id }}" id="q-{{ qv_id }}-{{ v }}" value="{{ v }}" data-ot-input{% unless editable %} disabled{% endunless %}{% if chosen_value == v %} checked{% endif %} /><label class="sr-only" for="q-{{ qv_id }}-{{ v }}">{{ qv.al_questiontext | escape }}: {{ glabels[forloop.index0] }}</label></td>
              {% endfor %}
            </tr>

          {% elsif rt == 120910003 %}
            {% comment %}
              Primary root cause. The document lays its nine causes out as a 3x3 grid under
              their own heading rather than as a row of an adjacent table, so it breaks out
              of the block's table into one of its own. The table is the autosave root: bind()
              looks for its inputs and its status line inside whatever carries data-ot-answer,
              and a table serves as well as a row.
            {% endcomment %}
              </tbody>
            </table>
            {% assign rcvalues = '120910320,120910321,120910322,120910323,120910324,120910325,120910326,120910327,120910328' | split: ',' %}
            {% assign rclabels = 'FactFind quality,Risk / capacity mismatch,Research / rationale,Charges / value,Client communication,Process / documentation,AML / CRA,Retirement Proposition,Adviser judgement' | split: ',' %}
            <table class="rootcause"
                   data-ot-answer
                   data-question-version="{{ qv_id }}"
                   data-response-id="{{ existing.al_responseid }}"
                   data-response-type="{{ rt }}"
                   data-column="choice">
              <tbody>
                <tr><th colspan="3">{{ qv.al_questiontext | escape }}{% if qv.al_ismandatory %}<span class="ot-required">Required</span>{% endif %}<span class="ot-answer__status" data-ot-status aria-live="polite"></span></th></tr>
                <tr>
                {% for v in rcvalues %}
                  <td><label class="opt wrap"><input type="radio" class="cc-box" name="q-{{ qv_id }}" value="{{ v }}" data-ot-input{% unless editable %} disabled{% endunless %}{% if chosen_value == v %} checked{% endif %} /> {{ rclabels[forloop.index0] }}</label></td>
                  {% if forloop.index == 3 or forloop.index == 6 %}</tr><tr>{% endif %}
                {% endfor %}
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
            <table class="meta">
              <tbody>

          {% else %}
            <tr data-ot-answer
                data-question-version="{{ qv_id }}"
                data-response-id="{{ existing.al_responseid }}"
                data-response-type="{{ rt }}"
                data-column="{{ column }}">
              <td class="{% if blk_layout == 'grid' %}label{% else %}lbl{% endif %}">{{ qv.al_questiontext | escape }}{% if qv.al_ismandatory %}<span class="ot-required">Required</span>{% endif %}<span class="ot-answer__status" data-ot-status aria-live="polite"></span></td>
              <td colspan="{{ ncols | minus: 1 }}">
              {% if column == 'text' %}
                {% if rt == 120910001 %}
                  <textarea data-ot-input{% unless editable %} disabled{% endunless %} aria-label="{{ qv.al_questiontext | escape }}">{{ existing.al_answertext | escape }}</textarea>
                {% else %}
                  <input type="text" data-ot-input{% unless editable %} disabled{% endunless %} value="{{ existing.al_answertext | escape }}" aria-label="{{ qv.al_questiontext | escape }}" />
                {% endif %}

              {% elsif column == 'date' %}
                <input type="date" data-ot-input{% unless editable %} disabled{% endunless %} value="{{ existing.al_answerdate | date: 'yyyy-MM-dd' }}" aria-label="{{ qv.al_questiontext | escape }}" />

              {% elsif column == 'choices' %}
                {% comment %}
                  Tax check reason (Q-TAX-01), the only multi-select in V8. The shape Liquid
                  gives a multi-select choice column is not documented (the Liquid objects
                  page covers single picklists only), and the label match this used to rely
                  on rendered no selection on a submitted review that held two values. So
                  the checked test accepts every shape the runtime could plausibly return:
                  a collection of option set value objects (folded into a |value| list), a
                  string of comma-separated values, or a string of labels. None of the five
                  values or labels is a substring of another.
                {% endcomment %}
                {% assign mvalues = '120910340,120910341,120910342,120910343,120910344' | split: ',' %}
                {% assign mlabels = 'LSA/LSDBA/TTFAC,Trust,IHT,Tax calculation,Other' | split: ',' %}
                {% assign chosen = '' %}
                {% for c in existing.al_answerchoices %}{% assign chosen = chosen | append: '|' | append: c.value | append: '|' %}{% endfor %}
                <div class="opts">
                  {% for opt in mvalues %}
                    {% assign optkey = '|' | append: opt | append: '|' %}
                    <label class="opt"><input type="checkbox" class="cc-box" data-ot-input value="{{ opt }}"{% unless editable %} disabled{% endunless %}{% if chosen contains optkey or existing.al_answerchoices contains opt or existing.al_answerchoices contains mlabels[forloop.index0] %} checked{% endif %} /> {{ mlabels[forloop.index0] }}</label>
                  {% endfor %}
                </div>

              {% else %}
                {% include 'OT Answer Options' response_type: rt selected: existing.al_answerchoice.value question: qv_id editable: editable %}
              {% endif %}
              </td>
            </tr>
          {% endif %}

          {% assign first_row = false %}
          {% endif %}
        {% endfor %}
        {% endfor %}

        {% comment %}
          Close the last block. This used to hang off forloop.last of the single row loop;
          with sections outer there is no one iteration that is reliably last, so it is done
          once both loops have finished. first_row is still true only when the team's form
          had no rows at all, in which case nothing was ever opened.
        {% endcomment %}
        {% unless first_row %}
          {% if blk_subsections and prev_help != blank %}
            <tr class="lens"{% if lens_tick_id != '' %} data-ot-answer data-question-version="{{ lens_tick_id }}" data-response-type="120910007" data-column="choice"{% endif %}><td colspan="{{ ncols | minus: lens_tick_span }}"><em>Outcome lens:</em> {{ prev_help | escape }}</td>{{ lens_tick_cell }}</tr>{% assign lens_tick_id = '' %}{% assign lens_tick_cell = '' %}{% assign lens_tick_span = 0 %}
          {% endif %}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        {% endunless %}
        </div>
        {% if editable %}
        <script>
          /*
           * Answer autosave and fail points (PP-07, PP-08, FR-013, AD-053, AD-096).
           *
           * Path: page -> Portals Web API PATCH al_reviewinstance.al_answerrequest ->
           * AnswerRequestPlugin -> AnswerWriter -> al_response -> ResponseGuardPlugin.
           *
           * This used to PATCH or POST al_response directly - a create binding two
           * @odata.bind lookups, a ticked reason a $ref associate/disassociate. Power
           * Pages refuses both with error 90040106, and no table permission value fixes
           * it; five were tried. So the page now writes one JSON payload (currentRequest())
           * onto the review instance's al_answerrequest column, which is allowlisted for
           * the Web API, and AnswerRequestPlugin parses it and calls AnswerWriter to do the
           * actual al_response create-or-update and reason reconciliation.
           *
           * This still needs no cloud flow. Submitting reaches Dataverse this same way now
           * too: SubmitRequestPlugin fires off one allowlisted boolean
           * (al_reviewinstance.al_submitrequested) rather than a Power Automate flow, the
           * same AD-053 trigger-column pattern used here - a different reason (the Web API
           * cannot invoke the al_SubmitReview action at all) landing on the same shape.
           * There is no flow run left to spare by debouncing; it now simply keeps a fast
           * typist from spending a synchronous plug-in execution on every keystroke.
           *
           * Every rule that matters - the submission lock, the AD-023 column match, the
           * option subset and AD-020 section ownership - is enforced in the plug-in. This
           * script is convenience, never access control (NFR-SEC-01).
           */
          (function () {
            'use strict';

            var DEBOUNCE_MS = 800;

            var shell = document.querySelector('[data-ot-questionnaire]');
            if (!shell) { return; }
            var REVIEW_ID = shell.getAttribute('data-review-id');
            if (!REVIEW_ID) { return; }

            /*
             * The anti-forgery token comes from shell.getTokenDeferred(), which is the
             * only supported way to obtain it and the way the claim and remediation
             * writes on this site already do it.
             *
             * It used to read a hidden [name="__RequestVerificationToken"] input instead,
             * and guard that read with `if (token)`. Power Pages renders that input on a
             * server-rendered form; this page is a hand-written template and carries no
             * form, so the element never existed, the guard silently dropped the header,
             * and every write left the browser unauthenticated. That is why the refusal
             * was 401 rather than the 403 a table permission produces - the request was
             * never attributed to the signed-in contact at all.
             */
            function api(method, path, body, onDone, onFail) {
              if (!window.shell || typeof window.shell.getTokenDeferred !== 'function') {
                onFail('', 0);
                return;
              }

              window.shell.getTokenDeferred().done(function (token) {
                var xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
                xhr.open(method, '/_api/' + path, true);
                xhr.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
                xhr.setRequestHeader('Accept', 'application/json');
                xhr.setRequestHeader('OData-MaxVersion', '4.0');
                xhr.setRequestHeader('OData-Version', '4.0');
                xhr.setRequestHeader('__RequestVerificationToken', token);
                // If-Match: * pins this PATCH to a row that already exists. Without it the
                // Web API treats the PATCH as an upsert, and the Review Instance - assigned
                // to me table permission grants write but not create, so the create half of
                // an upsert is refused. Every caller of api() now PATCHes an al_reviewinstance
                // row the page already loaded, so the header is always safe here - see the
                // submit block below (data-ot-submit script, "If-Match: * matters") for the
                // fuller account of why it exists and what broke without it.
                xhr.setRequestHeader('If-Match', '*');

                xhr.onload = function () {
                  if (xhr.status >= 200 && xhr.status < 300) {
                    onDone(xhr);
                  } else {
                    // The status travels with the body. A refusal carrying no plug-in prefix
                    // did not come from the plug-in at all, and only the status says whether
                    // it was the portal turning the write away or something transient.
                    onFail(xhr.responseText || xhr.statusText, xhr.status);
                  }
                };
                xhr.onerror = function () { onFail('', 0); };
                xhr.send(body ? JSON.stringify(body) : null);
              }).fail(function () { onFail('', 0); });
            }

            /*
             * The plug-in prefixes its failures so the client can branch without parsing
             * prose. Messages say what happened, what is still safe and what to do next,
             * and never expose a table name, query, id or stack trace (PP-16).
             *
             * The status is read as well as the body, because the most common failure here
             * never reaches the plug-in and so carries no prefix: the portal refuses the
             * write on the table permission chain and answers 403. "Not saved - retry" was
             * wrong for that case in the only way that matters - it is not going to work on
             * the retry, or the one after, and the person is given nothing to act on.
             *
             * The write path is Contact-scoped through contact_al_reviewinstance and carried
             * by the two checker web roles, so a 403 means one of exactly two things, and
             * both are worth naming rather than summarising as "permission".
             */
            /*
             * The server's own words, out of the response body.
             *
             * The body is an OData error object, so the message has to be read out of it rather than
             * scanned for in the raw text: a prefix found by indexOf and taken to the end of the
             * string carries the JSON tail with it, which is why the branches below used to cut at
             * the next quote and why one of them did not and showed it.
             *
             * A body that will not parse is returned as it stands - it is still more than "something
             * went wrong" - and it is collapsed onto one line and capped, because an error line is
             * read on screen and the whole of a platform fault is not readable there.
             */
            function serverMessage(raw) {
              var text = String(raw || '').trim();
              if (!text) { return ''; }

              var message = text;
              try {
                var body = JSON.parse(text);
                var error = body && body.error ? body.error : body;
                if (error && error.innererror && error.innererror.message) {
                  message = error.innererror.message;
                } else if (error && error.message) {
                  message = error.message;
                }
              } catch (ignored) {
                // Not JSON. Keep the raw text.
              }

              message = String(message || '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
              if (message.length > 300) { message = message.substring(0, 300) + '...'; }
              return message;
            }

            function friendlyError(raw, httpStatus) {
              var text = serverMessage(raw);
              if (text.indexOf('CONFLICT:') !== -1) {
                return 'This review has been submitted and can no longer be changed. Reload the page.';
              }
              if (text.indexOf('UNAUTHORIZED:') !== -1) {
                return 'You do not have permission to change this answer.';
              }
              if (text.indexOf('PRECONDITION:') !== -1) {
                // serverMessage has already taken the message out of the JSON, so this is
                // the message and nothing else. It used to be cut at the next quote to keep
                // the trailing JSON off the screen; that is now unnecessary.
                var idx = text.indexOf('PRECONDITION:');
                return text.substring(idx + 'PRECONDITION:'.length).trim();
              }
              if (text.indexOf('UNEXPECTED:') !== -1) {
                var unexpected = text.indexOf('UNEXPECTED:');
                return 'That answer was not saved. ' + text.substring(unexpected + 'UNEXPECTED:'.length).trim();
              }
              /*
               * 401 and 403 are deliberately not the same message. They were, and that
               * cost three rounds of investigation: a 401 was read as a table permission
               * refusal and sent everyone to look at web roles that were correct all
               * along. The portal answers 403 when the permission chain turns a write
               * away, and 401 when the request never authenticated - an expired session,
               * or a missing anti-forgery token.
               */
              if (httpStatus === 403) {
                return 'Your account is not allowed to record answers on this review, so nothing was saved. '
                  + 'Answering needs a Tax or AQS reviewer role on your portal account, and the review has to be '
                  + 'assigned to you. Ask an administrator to check both - retrying will not help.';
              }
              if (httpStatus === 401) {
                return 'Your session was not accepted, so nothing was saved. Reload the page and sign in again, '
                  + 'then check this answer is still on screen before carrying on.';
              }
              if (httpStatus === 0) {
                return 'That answer did not reach the server. Check your connection; it will save when you next change it.';
              }
              // The server's message rather than "retry": a refusal the page cannot
              // classify is exactly the one whose detail the reader needs.
              return 'Not saved. Your other answers are unaffected.'
                + (text ? ' The server said: ' + text : '')
                + (httpStatus ? ' (status ' + httpStatus + ')' : '');
            }

            // idFromHeader() used to read the new row's id back out of OData-EntityId
            // after a direct al_response create, so save() could remember it for the next
            // PATCH. There is no create here any more - AnswerWriter finds or creates the
            // row server-side - so nothing on this page needs the id, and the function is
            // gone with the create it served.

            function two(n) { return (n < 10 ? '0' : '') + n; }

            function bind(root) {
              var questionVersion = root.getAttribute('data-question-version');
              var column = root.getAttribute('data-column');
              var status = root.querySelector('[data-ot-status]');
              // The standalone fail points list (AD-096) belongs to the File quality outcome
              // answer, so only that answer's bind() finds one; every other answer has none.
              var reasons = document.querySelector('[data-ot-failpoints-for="' + questionVersion + '"]');
              var inputs = root.querySelectorAll('[data-ot-input]');
              var timer = null;

              function show(message, kind) {
                if (!status) { return; }
                status.textContent = message;
                status.className = 'ot-answer__status ot-answer__status--' + kind;
              }

              function chosenChoice() {
                for (var i = 0; i < inputs.length; i++) {
                  if (inputs[i].checked) { return parseInt(inputs[i].value, 10); }
                }
                return null;
              }

              function currentPayload() {
                var body = {};
                var i;

                if (column === 'text') {
                  body.al_answertext = inputs[0].value || null;
                } else if (column === 'date') {
                  body.al_answerdate = inputs[0].value || null;
                } else if (column === 'choice') {
                  body.al_answerchoice = chosenChoice();
                } else {
                  var picked = [];
                  for (i = 0; i < inputs.length; i++) {
                    if (inputs[i].checked) { picked.push(parseInt(inputs[i].value, 10)); }
                  }
                  // AnswerRequestPlugin's answerChoices is an int[] (AnswerRequest.cs,
                  // AnswerChoices), not the comma-separated value list this used to build -
                  // that format was for the OData multiselect column this page no longer
                  // writes directly. currentRequest() below sends this array through as-is.
                  body.al_answerchoices = picked.length ? picked : null;
                }

                return body;
              }

              /*
               * The answer and its reasons in one object. It travels as a JSON string on
               * al_answerrequest rather than as an al_response create, because Power Pages
               * refuses the create's @odata.bind association (90040106) whatever the table
               * permissions say. AnswerRequestPlugin does the write.
               */
              function currentRequest() {
                var answer = currentPayload();
                var request = {
                  questionVersionId: questionVersion,
                  answerText: answer.al_answertext === undefined ? null : answer.al_answertext,
                  answerChoice: answer.al_answerchoice === undefined ? null : answer.al_answerchoice,
                  answerChoices: answer.al_answerchoices === undefined ? null : answer.al_answerchoices,
                  answerDate: answer.al_answerdate === undefined ? null : answer.al_answerdate,
                  failReasons: selectedReasonIds(),
                  renderedReasons: renderedReasonIds()
                };
                return request;
              }

              function selectedReasonIds() {
                var boxes = reasons ? reasons.querySelectorAll('[data-ot-reason]') : [];
                var ids = [];
                for (var i = 0; i < boxes.length; i++) {
                  if (boxes[i].checked) { ids.push(boxes[i].value); }
                }
                return ids;
              }

              /*
               * Every reason checkbox the fail points block put on screen, ticked or not -
               * the category-filtered failreasons fetch (template ~line 264), not the full
               * catalogue. AnswerWriter.ReconcileFailReasons only ever disassociates an id
               * that is both in this list and missing from selectedReasonIds(); a reason
               * linked to the answer but outside this list (the other team's category) is
               * left alone, because its absent checkbox never gave the reviewer a chance to
               * untick it (finding 2, 2026-09-08 review).
               */
              function renderedReasonIds() {
                var boxes = reasons ? reasons.querySelectorAll('[data-ot-reason]') : [];
                var ids = [];
                for (var i = 0; i < boxes.length; i++) { ids.push(boxes[i].value); }
                return ids;
              }

              // Nothing on an answer is revealed conditionally any more. The evidence note
              // that revealConditional() used to open on a non-pass is not a field on the
              // Checker Checklist and has been removed, and the fail points block is always
              // on screen (AD-096), so every control an answer has is visible from the start.

              /*
               * One PATCH replaces the create/update pair that used to target al_response
               * directly. The create branch used to set two @odata.bind lookups
               * (al_reviewinstance_response, al_questionversion_response) so Power Pages
               * would associate the new row to its review and question version - and that
               * association is exactly what error 90040106 refuses, whatever the table
               * permissions say. This PATCHes the review instance's al_answerrequest column
               * instead; AnswerRequestPlugin parses the JSON and AnswerWriter does the
               * create-or-update against al_response server-side. The responseId branch and
               * the data-response-id bookkeeping that went with it go with it - there is no
               * create left here to remember an id for.
               */
              function save() {
                var body = { al_answerrequest: JSON.stringify(currentRequest()) };
                show('Saving...', 'saving');

                function done() {
                  var now = new Date();
                  show('Saved ' + two(now.getHours()) + ':' + two(now.getMinutes()), 'saved');
                }

                api('PATCH', 'al_reviewinstances(' + REVIEW_ID + ')', body,
                  done,
                  function (raw, httpStatus) { show(friendlyError(raw, httpStatus), 'error'); });
              }

              function queue() {
                if (timer) { window.clearTimeout(timer); }
                timer = window.setTimeout(save, DEBOUNCE_MS);
              }

              /*
               * A reason is now part of the answer payload, so ticking one is just another
               * change to save. The old "choose an answer first" rule went with the
               * association it protected.
               */
              function toggleReason() {
                queue();
              }

              for (var i = 0; i < inputs.length; i++) {
                inputs[i].addEventListener('change', queue);
                if (inputs[i].tagName === 'TEXTAREA' || inputs[i].type === 'text') {
                  inputs[i].addEventListener('input', queue);
                }
              }

              // No closure over the box is needed any more: toggleReason() no longer takes
              // one, since a tick is just another field feeding queue() -> save().
              var reasonBoxes = reasons ? reasons.querySelectorAll('[data-ot-reason]') : [];
              for (var r = 0; r < reasonBoxes.length; r++) {
                reasonBoxes[r].addEventListener('change', toggleReason);
              }
            }

            var answers = document.querySelectorAll('[data-ot-answer]');
            for (var i = 0; i < answers.length; i++) { bind(answers[i]); }
          })();
        </script>
        {% endif %}

      {% endif %}
    {% endif %}

    {% comment %}
      Remediation and escalation is NOT on this form.

      The document carries it as a closing block marked "(PICKED UP ON ANOTHER FORM)", and
      that is how it is built: al_RemediationAction, al_Signoff and al_Outcome, worked on the
      remediation page and the sign-off step, per case rather than per review (AD-095). This
      page used to render a read-only copy of it beneath the checklist; the project owner's
      direction on 2026-09-09 was that the checklist form is the checklist and remediation
      belongs to its own step, so the copy and its three fetches are gone. Nothing about the
      remediation data changed - only where it is read.
    {% endcomment %}

    {% comment %}
      Submit panel (PP-07, PP-11). Deliberately the last thing on the page.

      It used to sit directly under the summary card, above the questionnaire, so on a
      42-question checklist the button was off-screen by the time a reviewer had answered
      anything and submitting meant scrolling back up past every answer. Submitting is the
      end of the task, so it belongs at the end of the page - reached by finishing the
      questionnaire rather than by scrolling away from it.

      The button is only an affordance: the submission re-checks the transition, every
      mandatory question and the Tax-before-AQS rule server-side, so moving, hiding or
      showing it changes nothing about what is permitted (NFR-SEC-01).
    {% endcomment %}
    {% if editable %}
      <section class="ot-card ot-submit"
               data-ot-submit
               data-review-id="{{ rv.al_reviewinstanceid }}"
               aria-labelledby="ot-submit-heading">
        <h2 class="ot-card__title" id="ot-submit-heading">Submit this review</h2>
        <p>
          Submitting locks the review. Every question marked Required must be answered
          first; the check runs server-side, so a missing answer is refused rather than
          silently accepted.
        </p>
        <p>
          <button type="button" class="ot-btn ot-btn--primary" data-ot-submit-button>
            Submit review
          </button>
        </p>
        <p class="ot-submit__status" data-ot-submit-status aria-live="polite"></p>
      </section>
      <script>
        /*
         * Submit Review (PP-07, PP-08, PP-11).
         *
         * Path: review page -> PATCH al_reviewinstance -> SubmitRequestPlugin -> Dataverse.
         *
         * The portals Web API does not support calling actions, so the al_SubmitReview
         * Custom API cannot be invoked from a page. Rather than bridge that with a Power
         * Automate flow, the page sets one allowlisted boolean and a synchronous plug-in
         * does the work - the same mechanic the autosave above already uses for answers
         * (AD-053). Because the step is synchronous, a refusal comes back on this request
         * with its message intact.
         *
         * The request is built exactly like the autosave's api() helper, and that is the
         * point rather than a coincidence. Two writes to the same site over the same API
         * should not be assembled two different ways.
         *
         * They were once reconciled the other way round: this block took its token from
         * shell.getTokenDeferred() and was changed to read the autosave's hidden field
         * because the autosave was "known to work". It was not - no portal write path on
         * this site had ever been exercised by a signed-in user, and the hidden field the
         * autosave read has never existed on this page. Reconciling on an unproven
         * reference moved the working half onto the broken one. Both now use
         * getTokenDeferred(), which is the documented mechanism.
         *
         * If-Match: * matters. Without it a PATCH to the Dataverse Web API is an upsert,
         * and the table permission grants write but not create (see the Review Instance -
         * assigned to me row), so the create half of an upsert is refused. It pins this to
         * an update of a row that already exists.
         *
         * This script is progressive enhancement only. Every rule that matters - the
         * transition, mandatory questions, Tax before AQS, idempotency and the submission
         * lock - is enforced in the plug-in. Disabling the button here is a courtesy to the
         * user, never an access control (NFR-SEC-01).
         */
        (function () {
          'use strict';

          var root = document.querySelector('[data-ot-submit]');
          if (!root) { return; }

          var button = root.querySelector('[data-ot-submit-button]');
          var status = root.querySelector('[data-ot-submit-status]');
          var reviewId = root.getAttribute('data-review-id');

          if (!button || !reviewId) { return; }

          function show(message, kind) {
            if (!status) { return; }
            status.textContent = message;
            status.className = 'ot-submit__status ot-submit__status--' + (kind || 'info');
            status.setAttribute('role', kind === 'error' ? 'alert' : 'status');
          }

          /*
           * The plug-in prefixes its failures so the client can branch without parsing
           * prose. Messages here say what happened, what is still safe, and what to do
           * next, and never expose a table name, query, id or stack trace (PP-16).
           *
           * httpStatus is read as well as the body. A refusal that carries no prefix did
           * not come from the plug-in at all - it came from the portal, before Dataverse
           * was reached - and 401/403 there means the site would not accept the write
           * rather than that anything went wrong transiently. Telling that user to "try
           * again" sends them round a loop that cannot terminate, which is what the single
           * generic message used to do for every non-2xx alike.
           */
          /*
           * The server's own words, out of the response body.
           *
           * The body is an OData error object, so the message has to be read out of it rather than
           * scanned for in the raw text: a prefix found by indexOf and taken to the end of the
           * string carries the JSON tail with it, which is how a PRECONDITION message came to be
           * shown with '","innererror":{...}}' on the end of it.
           *
           * A body that will not parse is returned as it stands - it is still more than "something
           * went wrong" - and it is collapsed onto one line and capped, because an error line is
           * read on screen and the whole of a platform fault is not readable there.
           */
          function serverMessage(raw) {
            var text = String(raw || '').trim();
            if (!text) { return ''; }

            var message = text;
            try {
              var body = JSON.parse(text);
              var error = body && body.error ? body.error : body;
              if (error && error.innererror && error.innererror.message) {
                message = error.innererror.message;
              } else if (error && error.message) {
                message = error.message;
              }
            } catch (ignored) {
              // Not JSON. Keep the raw text.
            }

            message = String(message || '').replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();
            if (message.length > 300) { message = message.substring(0, 300) + '...'; }
            return message;
          }

          function friendlyError(raw, httpStatus) {
            var text = serverMessage(raw);
            if (text.indexOf('CONFLICT:') !== -1) {
              return 'This review changed since you opened it. Reload the page and try again - your answers are still saved.';
            }
            if (text.indexOf('UNAUTHORIZED:') !== -1) {
              return 'You do not have permission to submit this review.';
            }
            if (text.indexOf('PRECONDITION:') !== -1) {
              // Carries the actionable detail, e.g. how many required questions remain.
              var idx = text.indexOf('PRECONDITION:');
              return text.substring(idx + 'PRECONDITION:'.length).trim();
            }
            if (text.indexOf('UNEXPECTED:') !== -1) {
              var unexpected = text.indexOf('UNEXPECTED:');
              return 'This review was not submitted. ' + text.substring(unexpected + 'UNEXPECTED:'.length).trim();
            }
            if (httpStatus === 403) {
              /*
               * Reloading and trying once more was the wrong advice: this refusal is the
               * permission chain, and it will refuse again.
               *
               * It also used to promise "your answers are still saved", and that promise was
               * false in the case that produces it. Answering and submitting travel the same
               * Contact-scoped permission chain, so an account refused here was refused on
               * every autosave too - the answers were never written, and a reader who trusts
               * the message loses the lot on reload. Reported exactly that way. It now says
               * what is true, which is that it does not know.
               */
              return 'Your account is not allowed to submit this review, so nothing was submitted. Check your answers '
                + 'are still on screen before leaving the page - the same permission stops them being saved, so they '
                + 'may not have been. Submitting needs a Tax or AQS reviewer role on your portal account, and the '
                + 'review has to be assigned to you. Ask an administrator to check both rather than retrying.';
            }
            /*
             * Split from 403 for the reason given on the autosave's friendlyError: a 401
             * is the session, not the permission chain, and conflating them sent three
             * rounds of investigation at web roles that were correct.
             */
            if (httpStatus === 401) {
              return 'Your session was not accepted, so nothing was submitted. Reload the page and sign in again, '
                + 'then check your answers are still on screen before submitting.';
            }
            /*
             * The server's message rather than an invented sentence. "We could not submit
             * this review, please try again" was true of every refusal alike and told the
             * reader nothing they could act on or quote - which is what was reported on
             * 2026-09-10, against a refusal whose real cause the page had been handed and
             * discarded.
             */
            return 'This review was not submitted, and nothing has been changed. '
              + (text ? 'The server said: ' + text : 'The server gave no detail.')
              + (httpStatus ? ' (status ' + httpStatus + ')' : '');
          }

          function setBusy(busy) {
            button.disabled = busy;
            button.setAttribute('aria-busy', busy ? 'true' : 'false');
            button.textContent = busy ? 'Submitting...' : 'Submit review';
          }

          /*
           * What the server render does for a submitted review, done to the page in hand:
           * every control the questionnaire owns is disabled and the submit button stays
           * disabled, leaving the status line so the confirmation stays readable. The
           * autosave script sees disabled controls as nothing to save.
           */
          function lockPage() {
            var controls = document.querySelectorAll(
              '[data-ot-questionnaire] input, [data-ot-questionnaire] textarea, [data-ot-questionnaire] select, [data-ot-questionnaire] button');
            for (var i = 0; i < controls.length; i++) { controls[i].disabled = true; }
            button.disabled = true;
            button.textContent = 'Submitted';
            button.setAttribute('aria-busy', 'false');
          }

          button.addEventListener('click', function () {
            if (button.disabled) { return; }

            if (!window.shell || typeof window.shell.getTokenDeferred !== 'function') {
              show('This page did not load completely. Refresh and try again.', 'error');
              return;
            }

            setBusy(true);
            show('Submitting your review...', 'info');

            function unreachable() {
              show('The submission did not reach the server. Check your connection and try again - your answers are still saved.', 'error');
              setBusy(false);
            }

            // Power Pages requires the request verification token on every write, and
            // shell.getTokenDeferred() is the only supported way to obtain it. Same
            // mechanism the autosave above uses, so both writes present the same
            // credential - which is what the hidden-field read was meant to achieve and
            // did not, because this page renders no form to carry that field.
            window.shell.getTokenDeferred().done(function (token) {
              var request = new XMLHttpRequest();
              request.open('PATCH', '/_api/al_reviewinstances(' + reviewId + ')', true);
              request.setRequestHeader('Content-Type', 'application/json');
              request.setRequestHeader('Accept', 'application/json');
              request.setRequestHeader('OData-MaxVersion', '4.0');
              request.setRequestHeader('OData-Version', '4.0');
              request.setRequestHeader('If-Match', '*');
              request.setRequestHeader('__RequestVerificationToken', token);

              request.onload = function () {
                if (request.status >= 200 && request.status < 300) {
                  /*
                   * Locked here, in the page already on screen, rather than by reloading.
                   * Power Pages renders from a server-side cache that a website write clears
                   * only for the record it wrote; al_submittedon is stamped by
                   * SubmitRequestPlugin and every answer row was created by AnswerWriter, so
                   * a reload straight after the PATCH could render the pre-submit review as
                   * still editable with no answers on it (AD-094). Change tracking on the
                   * portal tables is what makes the next visit right; not reloading is what
                   * makes this one right.
                   */
                  lockPage();
                  show('Review submitted. This review is now locked; reopening or regrading is a T&C Manager action.', 'success');
                  return;
                }

                show(friendlyError(request.responseText, request.status), 'error');
                setBusy(false);
              };

              request.onerror = unreachable;

              request.send(JSON.stringify({ al_submitrequested: true }));
            }).fail(unreachable);
          });
        })();
      </script>
    {% endif %}
  {% endif %}
{% endif %}
{% endblock %}
"
}
